speechtext:
In reply to the hon. member for Cape Breton North-Victoria, the hon. member for Cumberland (Mr. Black) and also the hon. member for Cape Breton South (Mr. MAY 19, 1947
Inquiries oj the Ministry
Gillis), who I understand would want to ask a similar question had he been in the house, may I say that the proposed basis of settlement reached by the negotiating committee of the union and the company, which is subject to approval by the miners themselves, is as follows:
(a)	For continued operation of the mines until the end of the contract year, January 31, 1948;
(b)	An immediate wage rate increase of $1 per shift;
(c)	Cooperation by the union and the employees in every way possible to increase the tonnage production, equal at least to the pre-war rate of production by reducing absenteeism and increasing efficiency. In this connection, it is understood, of course, that to bring about this result there will have to be very close cooperation between the employer and the employees;
(d)	For a review of production at the end of a six months' period, at which time, if production has been increased to the stipulated level of the agreement, an additional forty cents per shift will be made to the employees;
(e)	For the settlement of other matters of a minor nature which were in dispute.
I sincerely hope that the miners themselves will vote in favour of acceptance of the terms of the settlement.

I notice that some of my hon. friends from Saskatchewan cannot bring themselves to applaud that statement, and I congratulate them on their fundamental honesty. I noticed that; anyone on this side of the house could see it. I say to my good friends from Saskatchewan who did not applaud, the problem is that the real bosses of the party are the ones who believe in this, and when they get into power this is what will happen, whether my hon. friends like it or not.
If I may be allowed to proceed, Mr. Speaker, I have considered this question of a bill of rights, and I say that these fundamental rights and freedoms should be protected in any way we find it possible to make them safer and more soundly established. I should like to go farther and endorse this second bill of rights which was given to the American people by that great humanitarian, the late President Roosevelt in his message to congress. Here is a report of what he said:
President Roosevelt outlined in his message to the United States congress, today "a second bill of rights"'-economic rights. He listed these rights as including:
The right to a useful and remunerative job in the industries, or shops or farms or mines of the nation;
The right to earn enough to provide adequate food and clothing and recreation;
Human Rights

The right of every farmer to raise and sell his products at a return which will give him and his family a decent living;
The right of every business man, large and small, to trade in an atmosphere of freedom from unfair competition and domination by monopolies at home or abroad.
An hon. MEMBER; Hear, hear.
Mr. TUCKER; I say "Hear, hear" to that too. One unfair competitor is a government which pays no taxes and does business itself at the expense of the people.

If the people have to keep a horde of people working at a loss and put others out of business, I do not know how that would benefit them. This report continues:
The right of every family to a decent home;
The right to adequate medical care and the opportunity to achieve and enjoy good health;
The right to adequate protection from the economic fears of old age, sickness, accident and unemployment;
The right to a good education.
All of these rights spell security. And after this war is won we must be prepared to move forward, in the implementation of these rights, to new goals of human happiness and well-being.
I say that the Liberal party is just as devoted to the achievement of that second bill of rights as any other party in this country. We do say, and we have proved by our record, that we have been more successful in bringing those rights to the individual man and woman. By our record we have shown that we are devoted to the second bill of rights. But in seeking that second bill of rights we should not abandon or endanger the ancient and primary rights and fundamental freedoms of our people. If we come to value security or well-being more than freedom or liberty we shall end up by losing both. On that basis I would suggest that this committee of this house study this great problem which lies before us and I am sure that, in doing so, they will make a great contribution to the economic well-being and to the preservation of the liberties of free Canadians.
